// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row vec_index_store_live_shared_loan_admits (tier 2).
// ILLEGAL and ADMITTED: `e` is a live shared loan of `v`, and the indexed store
// `v[*e as u64] = 9` needs `&mut v` while that loan is still read by the index
// expression. rustc 1.98.1 refuses with error[E0502] ("cannot borrow `v` as
// mutable because it is also borrowed as immutable", measured 2026-09-16 with
// --crate-name; the first measurement read REFUSED for a reason that had
// nothing to do with the program — the twin's FILENAME carried a dot and rustc
// rejected it as an invalid crate name). Logos compiles it and runs exit 9.
//
// ⚠ THE OBVIOUS ARM IS PRICED AND DECLINED, REASON 3 (its own cost is measured
// NON-ZERO). borrow_check.cpp::store_loans_die_in_stmt_ is the 2026-09-14b
// landing that reads an IndexMut store's `&mut v` autoref as a two-phase
// borrow. Disarming it refuses this program correctly — and ALSO refuses
// bc_0914b_ptrcoerceland_hb_v02_admit and bc_0914b_ptrcoerceland_hb_v02b_admit,
// two programs whose rustc twins were measured ACCEPTING. The query cannot tell
// "the loan's last use is this statement" from "the loan is read BY the store's
// own value expression", and `v.push(v.len())` — a METHOD RECEIVER's autoref,
// which really IS two-phase — must keep compiling. The repair is a narrowing at
// the same predicate (ask whether the conflicting loan is read by the INDEX or
// by the VALUE), not the disarm.
//
// This row replaces tests/logos/pass/bc_0914b_ptrcoerceland_hb_v02i_admit,
// which asserted `exit: 9` — a fixture may not keep asserting what rustc
// contradicts.
package vec_index_store_live_shared_loan_admits;
fn main() -> i32 {
    let mut v: Vec<i64> = Vec::new();
    v.push(1i64); v.push(0i64);
    let e: &i64 = &v[1u64];
    v[*e as u64] = 9i64;
    return v[0u64] as i32;
}
